    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so is it just like... servers waiting around? or is there some kind of actual process that moves the data from point a to point b? it feels kinda random when it hits some places but not others.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's a distributed caching process rather than one centralized transfer. When you change a record, only the authoritative DNS servers get the immediate update, but everyone else relies on upstream resolvers that cache the old data. When those caches expire, they then query the authoritative source for the latest record, leading to the seemingly random propagation you observe.",
      "source": "target",
